Porthole: A small, efficient port scanner

DESCRIPTION
Port scanner is a lightweight port scanner that
IT professionals can use to layout a network
attack surface and identify open/exposed network ports.

CONFIGURATION
Porthole can be configured with its configuration file, named 'porthole.conf'.
Here, you can alter the values of the fields to fit your needs:

1. port-range: the range of ports to scan for exposed services. It will scan the endpoint
ports as well, so for example, a port range of 1,1024 will can ports 1 to 1024, including 
1 and 2024.

2. threads: the of threads to use when executing Porthole. Multithreaded performance
will speed up the port scan and deliver results of scanning thousands of ports in
a matter of seconds as opposed to minutes.

3. max-bytes: the total number of bytes that should be received from the connecting user
in order to prevent an overload of unnecessary connection data. It is recommended to keep
this value as a power of 2 and not too high. by default, the maximum bytes that Microbait
will receive from connecting user is 64 KiB.

4. ips: the list of comma-separated IP addresses to perform a port scan on. The range
of ports scanned on each IP address will be that of the value held by the port-range variable.

GENERAL USE
In the directory you have downloaded Porthole in, run the command: “python3 porthole.py”. 
Ensure that porthole.conf is in the same directory as the script is.
Porthole will automatically start scanning ports on 1 IP at a time after the IP address
has been validated and its availability has been assessed. Upon completion on all IP
addresses specified, Porthole will create a list of ports identified as open and indicate
the time it took for the scan to complete. For most, if not all scans, it is expected that
the majority of ports scanned do not appear in the list of open ports, as this may indicate
a vulnerable machine that has several areas susceptible to a cyberattack or unauthorized access.

